<?php /* Template Name: Journal */ get_header(); ?><section class="editorial-heading"><p class="eyebrow">NOTES FROM OUR WORLD</p><h1>The <em>Journal.</em></h1><p>Thoughts on making, keeping, and finding beauty in the ordinary.</p></section><div class="journal-list"><article class="journal-feature"><div class="journal-photo journal-photo-one"></div><div><p class="eyebrow">MAKERS / 6 MIN READ</p><h2>A potter’s hands remember what the wheel forgets.</h2><p>In a small hillside studio, every bowl begins with a pause. We spent an afternoon with ceramicist Mara Ellis and left with clay beneath our nails.</p><details class="journal-read-more"><summary class="text-link">Read the story ↗</summary><div><p>Mara starts each morning by wedging yesterday’s clay, folding air and memory out of the material before it reaches the wheel. Her shelves are lined with pieces that almost worked: a rim that leaned too far, a glaze that settled in an unexpected way. Nothing is wasted; each attempt teaches the next.</p><p>When a bowl is finished, it carries the small differences of the hands that shaped it. That is the point. The pieces we return to are rarely identical, and never need to be.</p></div></details></div></article><article class="journal-feature reverse"><div class="journal-photo journal-photo-two"></div><div><p class="eyebrow">AT HOME / 4 MIN READ</p><h2>On setting a table for no particular reason.</h2><p>A Tuesday, a mismatched set of plates, something warm in the oven. The best rituals rarely need an occasion.</p><details class="journal-read-more"><summary class="text-link">Read the story ↗</summary><div><p>There is a particular ease to a table set without a plan. A linen runner that has softened with use, the plate you found at a market, a glass that does not match the others. They make room for the evening to be ordinary, which is often when it is most welcome.</p><p>Light a candle before the food is ready. Put the good bowl in the middle. Let dinner take as long as it takes. You do not need a reason to make a little space for yourself and the people you love.</p></div></details></div></article></div><section class="journal-note"><p class="eyebrow">A NOTE, EVERY SO OFTEN</p><h2>Good things to <em>read slowly.</em></h2><p>Join the demo newsletter to see the subscription flow. Addresses are stored privately in WordPress; no emails are sent.</p></section><?php get_footer(); ?>
